Subject: [flux] Make the console column and the avatar clickable

Two things stopped a click reaching them, and fixing only one changed
nothing visible.

First, the frame. Both views are children of BrowserView placed in the band
along the top and left that BrowserView::NonClientHitTest treats as titlebar.
On Windows that band answers WM_NCHITTEST with HTCAPTION, at which point the
system starts a window drag and never delivers a client mouse event at all.
They now claim HTCLIENT before that logic, the same shape upstream uses for
views living in the caption area. The whole of each view is client area
deliberately: the sidebar is a WebView whose every pixel is interactive, and
the avatar is a button. The tab strip beside them still drags the window.

Second, sibling order. The avatar is drawn inside the tab strip's band, and
Chromium adds the tab strip after it on purpose - it "needs to paint on top of
everything". Views hit-tests children in reverse order, so on top also means
it receives the mouse, and the tab strip region took every press aimed at the
avatar. With the frame fixed the window stopped dragging and the button still
did nothing, which is what made the second cause visible at all. Lifting the
avatar to the end of the child list puts it above the strip for targeting as
well as painting.

The reorder happens before horizontal_tab_strip_region_insertion_index_ is
recorded, because moving a child shifts the strip's index down by one and a
stale index is how it gets reinserted in the wrong place later.

--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
@@ -967,6 +967,21 @@
   // focus order but also needs to paint on top of everything.
   horizontal_tab_strip_region_view_ =
       AddChildView(CreateHorizontalTabStripRegionView(this));
+
+  // The Flux avatar is drawn inside the tab strip's band, and the tab strip is
+  // added after it precisely because it "needs to paint on top of everything".
+  // Views hit-tests children in reverse order, so on top also means it gets
+  // the mouse: the avatar was painted, visible, and unclickable, because the
+  // tab strip region sitting over it took every press. Lifting the avatar to
+  // the end puts it above the strip for both painting and targeting.
+  //
+  // Before the insertion index is recorded, not after: reordering shifts the
+  // tab strip down one, and a stale index is how the strip ends up reinserted
+  // in the wrong place on the next immersive transition.
+  if (flux_avatar_) {
+    ReorderChildView(flux_avatar_, -1);
+  }
+
   horizontal_tab_strip_region_insertion_index_ =
       GetIndexOf(horizontal_tab_strip_region_view_.get());
 
@@ -4591,6 +4606,32 @@
   views::View::ConvertPointToTarget(parent(), this,
                                     &point_in_browser_view_coords);
 
+  // Flux's own frame furniture claims its hits before anything below can call
+  // the area caption.
+  //
+  // The console column and the account avatar are children of the browser
+  // view that occupy the band this function otherwise treats as titlebar. On
+  // Windows that band answers WM_NCHITTEST with HTCAPTION, so the system
+  // begins a window drag and NO client mouse event is ever delivered - the
+  // avatar's menu never opened and the sidebar's own buttons did nothing, for
+  // a reason no amount of reading their click handlers could reveal.
+  //
+  // The whole of each view is client area on purpose: the sidebar is a WebView
+  // and every pixel of it is interactive, and the avatar is a button. The tab
+  // strip to their right is still there to drag the window by.
+  if (flux_sidebar_ && flux_sidebar_->GetVisible()) {
+    gfx::Point test_point(point);
+    if (ConvertedHitTest(parent(), flux_sidebar_, &test_point)) {
+      return HTCLIENT;
+    }
+  }
+  if (flux_avatar_ && flux_avatar_->GetVisible()) {
+    gfx::Point test_point(point);
+    if (ConvertedHitTest(parent(), flux_avatar_, &test_point)) {
+      return HTCLIENT;
+    }
+  }
+
   // Check if the point is in the web_app_frame_toolbar_. Because this toolbar
   // can entirely be within the window controls overlay area, this check needs
   // to be done before the window controls overlay area check below.
